**[minor/bug]** Nested ternary (3 levels) + semantically off. `keySet` only checks the canonical env var (`API_KEYS[name]`), so for google a key that came from `GEMINI_API_KEY` falls into the `effective` branch and `cfg.sources.apiKey === "env"` → prints the meaningless label `"KEY SET (resolved)"` — exactly the case the per-provider env layer was added for. Also `cfg.sources.apiKey === "user"` is only possible when `[llm].env_key` is set, so "env_key" is right, but the generic fallback hides the name. Flatten it and name the actual variable.

```suggestion
let where: string;
      if (keySet) {
        where = "KEY SET (" + apiKey + ")";
      } else if (effective) {
        where = "KEY SET (" + (cfg.sources.apiKey === "credentials" ? "stored file" :
          cfg.sources.apiKey === "user" ? "env_key" : "env") + ")";
      } else if (fromStore.length > 0) {
        where = "KEY SET (stored file)";
      } else {
        where = "NO KEY";
      }
```

**[minor/bug]** `stored.length > 0` only proves a stored entry exists for this provider; no endpoint comparison was done here. When `name !== cfg.provider` the stored key is simply not eligible (wrong provider), and even when it is the selected provider the mismatch could be the endpoint *or* the provider binding. The unconditional "the effective endpoint differs" asserts a cause that was never checked — misleading on the main troubleshooting surface. Report the stored bindings without claiming a reason, or only add the endpoint reason when `name === cfg.provider`.

```suggestion
if (!set && stored.length > 0) {
        console.log("      stored for: " + stored.join(", ") + " — not used by the current config");
      }
```

**[info/performance]** `storedBindings(name)` calls `readUserCredentials()`, which does `existsSync` + `readFileSync` + `JSON.parse` on every call. In this loop that is one full re-read/re-parse of the credential file per provider (3x here, plus once per `MODELS` entry in the `show` branch). Read it once and reuse.

```suggestion
const cfg = resolveLlmConfig();
    const credFile = readUserCredentials();
    for (const [name, factoryFn] of Object.entries(PROVIDER_FACTORIES)) {
      const apiKey = API_KEYS[name] || "UNKNOWN";
      const keySet = !!process.env[apiKey];
      const fromStore = credFile.bindings
        .filter((b) => b.provider === name)
        .map((b) => b.endpoint ?? "(provider default endpoint)");
```

**[minor/test]** No guard that `ANS_LLM_BASE_URL` is actually set. If it's undefined this passes `--endpoint undefined`, and both the bind and the later exact-match `auth remove --endpoint` assertions become vacuous (remove of `undefined` could match/delete anything, or fail for the wrong reason). Assert it's defined before using it as the binding key.

```suggestion
const credEndpoint = llmEnv.ANS_LLM_BASE_URL;
      check(typeof credEndpoint === "string" && credEndpoint.length > 0,
        "车道环境里 ANS_LLM_BASE_URL 必须已设置", String(credEndpoint));
```

**[minor/bug]** The directory is only chmod-restored after the spawn, not in a `finally`. If the spawn throws (spawn error, test abort, uncaught rejection) the 0o500 dir stays in `work` and any subsequent `rmSync(work)` teardown may fail on a non-writable dir. Wrap the chmod restore (and the temp home cleanup) in `try/finally`.

```suggestion
const tightHome = join(work, "tight-home");
        mkdirSync(join(tightHome, ".anysearch-cli"), { recursive: true });
        chmodSync(join(tightHome, ".anysearch-cli"), 0o500);
        try {
          // ... spawn ...
        } finally {
          chmodSync(join(tightHome, ".anysearch-cli"), 0o700);
        }
```
